Agent Name: agent_3
Step Number: 3
Reason for Mistake: In step 3, agent_3 correctly identifies that the salts NH₄NO₂ and NH₄NO₃ (total 17 atoms) result in a precise mass match of 7.20 g (0.05 mol each: 0.05×64 + 0.05×80 = 7.20 g). However, it rejects this chemically and experimentally valid combination. Instead, it concludes that the solution is 13 atoms (Option A), based on the assumption that NH₄NO₃ decomposes to N₂O (which does not produce O₂, contradicting the observed 0.025 mol of O₂) or errors in evaluating the mass consistency of the alternative pair (NaNO₃ + NH₄NO₂). The correct logic, consistent with the data (perfect mass balance and O₂ production), is 17 atoms (Option B). By rejecting the pair that fits the mass data perfectly and accepting the pair that fits the mass data approximately but requiring an error in stoichiometry (mass discrepancy), agent_3 made a fundamental error in prioritizing theoretical decomposition pathways (yielding N₂O) over the quantitative evidence (O₂ production and mass match).

\boxed{agent_3}
\boxed{3}
\boxed{Reason: Agent 3 identified the chemically and experimentally consistent pair (NH₄NO₂ + NH₄NO₃) via mass balance (7.20 g) but rejected it based on the assumption that NH₄NO₃ yields N₂O, failing to recognize that the decomposition producing N₂ and O₂ (which fits the data) would make 17 atoms the correct answer. This reasoning error led to the selection of the incorrect answer (13).}
